          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p28\",\"quote\":\"Simulations and a discontinuity in spin rate and spectral properties suggest that asteroids larger than approximately 120 km (75 mi) in diameter accreted during that early era, whereas smaller bodies are fragments from collisions between asteroids during or after the Jovian disruption.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p32\",\"quote\":\"Over 200 asteroids are known to be larger than 100 km, and a survey in the infrared wavelengths has shown that the asteroid belt has between 700,000 and 1.7 million asteroids with a diameter of 1 km or more.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p49\",\"quote\":\"The mass of all the objects of the asteroid belt , lying between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter , is estimated to be (2394 ± 6) × 10 18 kg , ≈3.25% of the mass of the Moon.\"}]}"
